[interviewer · INTRO] Your client is Brew & Bean, a specialty coffee chain with 200 locations across the US. Over the past two years, their net profit margin has declined from 24% to 6%, even though revenues have grown 15% over the same period. The CEO has hired us to identify the root cause of this profit decline and recommend how to reverse it. How would you approach this problem?

Take a minute if you need it, then tell me how you'd structure your approach.

[candidate silent 25s]

[candidate · 1:55] Okay. So revenue is actually up 15% but margin collapsed from 24% to 6% — that's a huge drop, so this is almost certainly a cost story rather than a demand story, though I want to confirm revenue quality too.

I'd structure it as profit = revenue minus costs, and go down both sides.

On revenue: price versus volume versus mix. Revenue growing 15% could be new store openings rather than same-store growth, which matters a lot — if they added locations, revenue grows mechanically while profit per store falls.

On costs: split fixed and variable. Variable would be coffee beans, milk, cups, hourly labor. Fixed would be rent, salaried staff, equipment, corporate overhead, and any new-store buildout costs.

I'd start on the cost side since that's where the 18 points went. The useful thing to begin with is a cost breakdown as a percent of revenue, two years ago versus today.
