[interviewer · CLARIFY · 1:57] Okay. Hold that request; I'll come back to it shortly. Is that structure MECE, and what, if anything, is missing from it?

[candidate · 3:27] Mostly, but there are gaps. I said net profit margin, and my structure is really an operating-level framework — I've left out everything below the line. Interest expense matters if they financed expansion with debt, plus depreciation on new buildouts, taxes, and any one-time charges like impairments or lease writedowns. With 200 locations and growth, debt service is a real candidate.

The other thing missing is the unit of analysis. I framed it chain-wide, but I should separate store-level P&L from corporate overhead, and within stores, separate mature stores from new ones. A new store can drag margin for a year or two before it matures, and if they've opened a lot recently, blended margin falls even with healthy economics.

On MECE — price, volume, mix overlaps a bit, since mix is a form of price. I'd clean that to revenue per transaction times number of transactions, with mix sitting underneath revenue per transaction.
