[interviewer · ANALYSIS · 3:29] COGS is 58% of revenue today, up from 42% two years ago. Labor is 22% of revenue, and it’s been stable. Overhead is 14% of revenue, up slightly from 12%. Okay. That's the cost breakdown you asked for. What do you take from it, and where do you dig next?

[candidate · 4:59] That accounts for essentially the whole thing. Two years ago: 42 plus 22 plus 12 is 76, leaving 24% margin. Today: 58 plus 22 plus 14 is 94, leaving 6%. So the 18-point drop is 16 points of COGS and 2 of overhead. Overhead is noise by comparison — COGS is the case.

A couple of things that follow. Labor holding flat at 22% while revenue grew 15% actually suggests they're running stores reasonably efficiently, so this isn't an operations-productivity story. And COGS as a percent of revenue rising means input costs outran whatever they charge, which points to either unit cost inflation or pricing that didn't move.

I'll take it that menu prices were held roughly flat, since that's the common instinct in specialty coffee — fear of losing traffic.

Where I want to go next is inside that 58%: beans versus dairy versus packaging and cups versus food items. One line is likely doing most of the damage.
