[candidate · 6:31] Fair — I flagged it as an assumption and I should test it rather than build on it.

Cleanest test is revenue per transaction over the two years. If I have transaction counts and revenue by year, I can back into average ticket. If ticket is flat while revenue grew 15%, growth came from volume or new stores, and prices didn't move. If ticket rose, prices or mix moved, and I'd need to separate those.

Separating them is the harder part. Ticket can rise because they raised prices or because customers traded up to more expensive drinks. So I'd want same-item pricing — what a 12-ounce latte cost two years ago versus today — at a handful of comparable stores. That isolates price from mix.

The other angle is to look at it the opposite direction: if COGS per unit rose 16 points and nothing was passed through, that's consistent with flat pricing. If input costs rose more than 16 and they did pass some through, pricing moved.

Do we have ticket by year?
