[interviewer · ANALYSIS · 6:33] The average transaction is $6.80, up from $6.20 two years ago — about a 10% increase, because customers are buying more items per visit. Menu prices have not changed in two years. You also asked about the COGS breakdown earlier. Which of those lines would you look at first, and why?

[candidate · 8:03] Good — so prices genuinely haven't moved in two years, and the ticket increase is all items-per-visit. That actually makes the COGS picture worse than it looks, because more items per visit means more cost per visit too, and none of it is margin they captured through price.

Within the 58%, I'd go to green coffee beans first. Two reasons. It's almost certainly the largest single line in a specialty coffee P&L, and it's an exchange-traded commodity with real volatility — arabica has had major run-ups. A big move there flows straight through with no pricing offset. Dairy is second for the same logic, though a smaller share.

Packaging and food I'd rank below those. Packaging is small as a percent, and food items are higher-cost but if food grew as a share of mix, that's a mix shift rather than inflation, which is a different fix.

So: bean cost per pound, two years ago versus today. And whether they're buying spot or contracted.
