[interviewer · EXHIBIT · 8:05] Raw coffee bean costs are up 40% over the past two years. Coffee beans were 25% of COGS two years ago. Other input costs — dairy, packaging, and food — are up 37.5% over the past two years. I don't have the spot versus contracted split. Using those figures, how much of the 16-point COGS increase do beans explain?

[candidate · 9:35] Let me work it. Two years ago COGS was 42 points of revenue. Beans at 25% of that is 10.5 points, everything else is 31.5 points.

Beans up 40%: 10.5 times 1.4 is 14.7, so an increase of 4.2 points.
Everything else up 37.5%: 31.5 times 1.375 is 43.3, an increase of 11.8 points.

Total today: 14.7 plus 43.3 is 58.0. That ties exactly to the 58% you gave me, which is a good sign the numbers are internally consistent.

So beans explain 4.2 of the 16 points — about a quarter. Dairy, packaging and food together explain roughly 11.8 points, three-quarters of it.

That reframes the case for me. I came in expecting beans to be the villain because it's the headline commodity, but the real story is broad-based input inflation across everything else, and that bucket is three times the size of the bean line to begin with.

One caveat — this math assumes the quantity of inputs per dollar of revenue held constant, so it's pure price inflation. But items per visit went up, which could mean the mix shifted toward lower-margin items too. I'd want to know which of dairy, packaging and food moved most.
