[interviewer · RECOMMENDATION · 11:11] Average revenue per store is available, so I'll give you that. The cut by location isn't in the information I have. Of everything you've laid out, which one lever would you take first, and what's your recommendation to the CEO? Average revenue per store is $2.4M a year.

[candidate · 12:41] My recommendation is price. Take a targeted menu price increase of roughly 5–8% in the next quarter.

Sizing it: 200 stores at $2.4M is $480M in revenue. At 6% that's about $29M of profit today. A 5% price rise on flat volume adds roughly $24M of near-pure flow-through, taking margin to about 11%; 8% gets them closer to 13%. Nothing else on my list moves that fast or that far.

Three supports. Prices have been frozen two years while inputs rose 37–40%, so they've absorbed the entire shock. Ticket is already up 10% on items per visit, which says demand is healthy and customers aren't price-fatigued. And labor holding at 22% means there's no efficiency slack to harvest instead.

Risks: volume response is the real one — I've assumed flat, and I'd want elasticity from a pilot in a subset of stores before chain-wide rollout. Specialty coffee is somewhat insulated, but 8% is not trivial.

Next steps: pilot pricing across maybe 20 stores for six weeks, and in parallel start supplier negotiations and hedging on the non-bean inputs, since that's where three-quarters of the damage sits. What about last year — whether any competitor has already moved on price would sharpen the elasticity read.
